---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/site-layout.astro';
import Bird from '../assets/bird.svg';
import factorial from '../assets/factorial.bd?raw';
import ExampleBrowser from '../components/example-browser.astro';
import { birdieGrammar } from '../lib/birdie-grammar';
---

<SiteLayout>
  <section class="hero wrap" aria-labelledby="hero-heading">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="hero-kicker">The Birdie programming language</p>
      <h1 id="hero-heading">A language<br />for algorithms<span class="red-period">.</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-description">Birdie is an experimental language for coding challenges and interview problems. It uses indentation for blocks, and its design includes static typing and built-in stacks and maps.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs">Read the guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href="#examples">See the code <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-sample code-sheet">
      <figcaption class="code-sheet-header filename">factorial.bd</figcaption>
      <Bird class="hero-bird" aria-hidden="true" />
      <div class="code-scroll">
        <Code code={factorial.trimEnd()} lang={birdieGrammar} themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' }} defaultColor={false} />
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="examples" class="examples-section wrap" aria-labelledby="examples-heading">
    <ExampleBrowser />
  </section>

  <section class="language-direction wrap" aria-labelledby="direction-heading">
    <div class="direction-intro">
      <h2 id="direction-heading">Syntax and<br />built-ins.</h2>
      <p>The examples drive the language design. They cover map lookups, stack operations, binary search, and grouping.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="direction-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Types and inference</dt>
        <dd>Function parameters have explicit types. Local variables, return types, and collection types can be inferred from use.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Built-in collections</dt>
        <dd>Lists, maps, and stacks require no imports. Maps can supply a default value or call a factory for missing keys.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Operation counts</dt>
        <dd>The planned scoring mode would count comparisons, collection operations, and allocations, including work done by built-ins.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="next-section wrap" aria-labelledby="next-heading">
    <div class="next-note">
      <h2 id="next-heading">Implementation<br />status.</h2>
      <p>Try integer and boolean expressions, assignments, branches, and functions in the REPL or the <a href="/playground">browser playground</a>.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/roadmap">Roadmap <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
    <a class="contribute-link" href="https://github.com/birdie-lang/birdie">
      <span>Language repository</span><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </a>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
